WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.510
Over the years, there have been many promising
actors who suffered untimely deaths.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:08.400
Though their causes of death may vary, all
of them are tragic examples of lives lost

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.400
too soon.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.280
Here are some actors who died shortly after
they became famous.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.190
Brad Renfro started acting before he even
hit his teens, and he made more than 20 movies

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.680
over the course of his career.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.780
He began his career in 1994, when he was only
10, playing a witness in a Mafia case in Joel

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.310
Schumacher's "The Client."

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.430
His other notable films include "The Cure,"
"Tom and Huck," "Sleepers," "Apt Pupil," and

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.430
"Bully."

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.541
Over his short career, Renfro worked with
some of Hollywood's biggest directors, like

00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:37.859
Barry Levinson and Bryan Singer, and acted
alongside some of the greatest actors in the

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:40.230
world, solidifying his place in movie history.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.579
Unfortunately, early fame led to Renfro growing
up very quickly, and the actor started using

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:46.469
drugs when he was quite young.

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:50.829
According to People, Renfro was first arrested
on drug charges in 1998, when he was only

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:51.829
16.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:55.719
He continued to have trouble with drugs and
the law throughout the rest of his life.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:01:00.989
In 2008, Renfro died of "acute heroin/morphine
intoxication" in what was later ruled an accidental

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:01.989
overdose.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:02.989
He was 25 at the time.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.630
A recently-released HBO documentary called
"What Happened, Brittany Murphy?"

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.840
delves deep into the mysterious death of Brittany
Murphy, who passed away suddenly in December

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.200
2009 — five months before her widower's
own mysterious death.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.110
Official records show her cause of death as
the result of a combination of untreated pneumonia,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.579
anemia, and a number of prescription and over-the-counter
drugs.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:27.729
Nonetheless, her death has been surrounded
by rumors for years, much like her short life

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:32.119
was plagued by rumors of eating disorders,
drug usage, and domestic drama.

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:36.440
Murphy made her professional acting debut
in 1991, and she quickly started landing gigs

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.899
on television shows such as "Drexell's Class"
and "Sister, Sister."

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:44.909
The actress gained higher notoriety with her
turn as Tai in 1995's iconic "Clueless," and

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:48.760
followed with solid supporting work in Hollywood
dramas including "Girl, Interrupted", the

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.640
Eminem-fronted "8 Mile," and "Don't Say A
Word," which earned her a Satellite Award

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.770
nomination.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.200
Murphy began landing lead roles with films
like the 2003 dramedy "Uptown Girls" and the

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.320
hit romantic comedy "Just Married," in which
she starred with then-boyfriend Ashton Kutcher.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.259
In addition to her on screen roles, Murphy
was known for voicing Luanne Platter in over

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.660
250 episodes of "King of the Hill" on FOX.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.520
"I think God has a plan for me.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.159
And it involves puppets."

00:02:13.159 --> 00:02:17.610
Her final role was in "Something Wicked,"
released five years after Murphy's death.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:23.530
Fans were heart-broken when Heath Ledger died
in January 2008, at the height of his fame.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.340
Toxicologists determined that Ledger died
of an accidental overdose, as the result of

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.390
abusing prescription medications.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.319
Only 28 years old at the time of his death,
he was on the cusp of releasing "The Dark

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.790
Knight," which ended up earning Ledger an
Oscar for his performance as The Joker.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.130
"It's a funny world we live in.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.510
Speaking of which, you know how I got these
scars?"

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.720
The Australian actor first made his mark in
1999, when he appeared as bad boy Patrick

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.420
Verona in the teen film "10 Things I Hate
About You."

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.780
From there, he quickly transitioned into more
serious parts, starring in films such as "The

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.879
Patriot," "Monster's Ball," "The Brothers
Grimm," and "The Four Feathers."

00:02:55.879 --> 00:02:59.700
Including posthumous releases, Ledger accumulated
23 acting credits.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.519
He received widespread acclaim for his role
in "Brokeback Mountain," in which he starred

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:08.500
as a married cowboy who had a secret gay relationship
in 1960s and '70s Wyoming.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.150
For the role, Ledger earned an Academy Award
nomination, a BAFTA nomination, a Golden Globe

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.420
nomination, and a Screen Actors Guild nomination,
among other awards.

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:20.959
Not to be confused with his son — actor
Freddie Prinze Jr. — Freddie Prinze was

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.381
a groundbreaking Puerto Rican comedian and
actor who gained fame in the 1970s after a

00:03:25.381 --> 00:03:29.280
series of buzz-worthy appearances on Johnny
Carson's "The Tonight Show."

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.080
Most people remember him from his role in
"Chico and the Man," an NBC vehicle built

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.250
around Prinze.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.540
On the series, Prinze played Chico Rodriguez,
an optimistic young Latino who worked alongside

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.930
a cranky old garage owner in East Los Angeles.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:45.530
The show was notable for offering a rare primetime
representation of Latinos, and was the first

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.030
sitcom to have been set in a Mexican-American
neighborhood.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:52.170
Prinze suffered with depression, and shot
himself at the age of 22.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.569
Doctors fought to save his life for 33 hours
before the actor passed away.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:58.140
One of his friends told The Los Angeles Times:

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.230
"People don't seem to be able to understand
that you can be talented, good-looking, healthy,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.870
that you could have fame and fortune and still
have a problem."

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.590
Prinze's son was not even a year old when
he passed, but he would become a celebrity

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.700
in his own right in films like "She's All
That" and "Summer Catch."

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.720
Canadian actor Cory Monteith became a star
due to his work on FOX's "Glee," where he

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.829
played sensitive jock Finn Hudson.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.850
Though he played a teenager, Monteith was
actually 27 when "Glee" started in 2009.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.320
Though he was not a household name before
"Glee," Monteith had appeared in a recurring

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.729
capacity on the series "Kyle XY" and in TV
movies like "Killer Bash".

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:36.310
In July 2013 — four years into the run of
"Glee" — Monteith was found dead in a hotel

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.870
room in Vancouver.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.660
Just 31 years old at the time of his death,
the autopsy would later report that Monteith

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.669
had died of, quote, "mixed drug toxicity,
involving heroin and alcohol."

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:49.340
In the aftermath of his passing, the "Glee"
cast and crew presented a tribute episode

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.039
entitled "The Quarterback," in which viewers
saw the aftermath of Monteith's character's

00:04:53.039 --> 00:04:54.720
death by unspecified causes.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.530
The "Glee" family has suffered more loss than
just Monteith — and Naya Rivera's 2020 death

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:04.960
was particularly tragic because it resulted
in her leaving a young son behind.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.860
Headlines were made when Rivera's four-year-old
son was found alone on a rented pontoon boat

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.419
at Lake Piru in California.

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:15.030
Fans took to social media hoping for Rivera's
safe return, as friends and family gathered

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.870
to help officials with the search.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.930
After several tense days, Rivera's body was
ultimately found less than a week later, with

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.830
the assumption that she saved her son from
drowning but wasn't able to save herself.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.889
Rivera had been a working actress for essentially
her entire life, starring in commercials as

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:31.270
a baby and then becoming a child star.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:35.560
As a kid, she appeared on programs like "Family
Matters," "Smart Guy," and "The Royal Family,"

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:36.699
on which she had a main role.

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:40.532
It was not until "Glee" that Rivera became
a household name, however, thanks to her role

00:05:40.532 --> 00:05:42.590
as snarky cheerleader Santana Lopez.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:47.150
When she died, Rivera was between seasons
on the TV show "Step Up: High Water."

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:52.389
In addition to acting, she also released music
and wrote a book in the years before her death.

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:56.550
Cameron Boyce was a rising child actor in
the process of making the difficult transition

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:01.620
to an adult career when he unexpectedly passed
away in his sleep in July 2019.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.260
Boyce's family later told the media that he
died after having a seizure, as the result

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.260
of epilepsy.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:10.370
He had just turned 20 a couple of months prior,
and had filmed interviews for Alex Winter's

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.970
"Showbiz Kids," an HBO documentary that examined
the treacherous bridge between child and adult

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:15.970
acting.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:19.610
Boyce's first role was in a music video for
the band Panic! at the Disco.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.740
In 2011, Boyce was cast in a lead role on
the Disney Channel's "Jessie," which ran for

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:28.240
98 episodes across four seasons and led to
further Disney work in the popular "Descendants"

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.110
franchise; Boyce also appeared in the "Grown
Ups" movies, and provided voice work on "Jake

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.900
and the Never Land Pirates" and "Descendants:
Wicked World."

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:39.410
Much of his adult acting work would be released
posthumously, including the HBO miniseries

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:43.620
"Mrs. Fletcher," the musical drama series
"Paradise City," and the thriller film "Runt."

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.380
The Phoenix family boasts a well-known Hollywood
bloodline, with Joaquin as the most successful

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:49.380
of the siblings.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.880
That said, it was River Phoenix who appeared
to be on the path to superstardom when he

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.710
died young in 1993, having headlined several
hit films.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.410
The older Phoenix died at age 23 during a
night partying at the famed Viper Room in

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:04.070
Hollywood, due to an overdose on cocaine and
heroin.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.729
Like his brother, Phoenix started in commercials
and performed throughout childhood.

00:07:07.729 --> 00:07:11.610
His big breakthrough came in Rob Reiner's
iconic film "Stand By Me".

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.320
"No one even asked me if I took the milk money
that time.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.270
I just got a three day vacation."

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:21.389
His star continued to rise as he appeared
in 1986's "The Mosquito Coast," and 1988's

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:24.800
"A Night in the Life of Jimmy Reardon".

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.460
After this, he garnered an Oscar nomination
for Sidney Lumet's powerful 1989 family drama

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.550
"Running on Empty," and then stood out as
young Indiana Jones in the opening flashback

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.860
of "Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade."

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:40.289
More classics followed in the next few years,
including Gus Van Sant's "My Own Private Idaho"

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.070
and 1992's lighthearted hacker drama "Sneakers".

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.479
The fact is, nobody knows what Rebecca Schaeffer's
career would look like today had the young,

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:52.050
talented actress not been killed just as her
career seemed to be blossoming.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:56.199
In a headline-grabbing tragedy, the "My Sister
Sam" star was shot on her own doorstep by

00:07:56.199 --> 00:08:01.520
obsessed fan Robert John Bardo in 1989, with
her tragic death leading to the first anti-stalking

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.380
law in the United States.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.440
Schaeffer was only 21 when her life was cut
short.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.159
Schaeffer started out as a teen model.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:12.389
She then had a few minor film roles before
breaking out with "My Sister Sam," a sitcom

00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:16.509
that cast her alongside TV favorite Pam Dawber
as the teenage sister who moved in and had

00:08:16.509 --> 00:08:18.470
begun wreaking havoc on her life.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:21.879
Schaeffer's other credits included "Out of
Time," "The End of Innocence," and "Scenes

00:08:21.879 --> 00:08:25.980
from the Class Struggle in Beverly Hills,"
a 1989 film that reportedly served as the

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:28.720
catalyst for Bardo's deranged obsession with
the actress.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.970
Out of all the stars on this list, James Dean
is perhaps the most iconic, an enduring symbol

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.710
of young stardom and a career ultimately cut
short by tragedy.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:42.280
Six decades after his death, Dean is still
a towering topic of Hollywood legend, inspiring

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.640
discussion, speculation and arguments about
everything from his sexuality to talk of whether

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.700
he really had the goods to turn his raw talent
into a lengthy, flourishing career.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.490
Despite his young death — or perhaps because
of it — Dean has become a Hollywood legend

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.320
whose legacy persists.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:00.720
Dean began his career by way of a 1950 Pepsi
commercial, but his acting chops and anguished

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.650
features catapulted him to fame.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:06.800
He only lived long enough to star in three
films — two of which were released posthumously

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.180
— and they all became classics: 1955's "East
of Eden," the same year's "Rebel Without a

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.430
Cause," and 1956's "Giant."

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:19.570
Dean died in a car accident in September 1955,
at the age of 24.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:23.270
Alongside Marlon Brando, James Dean may have
influenced more actors in each subsequent

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.020
generation — and helped pioneer an acting
sea change that valued authenticity over theatricality

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.950
— than any other Hollywood star.

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:35.230
Unlike the others on this list, Chadwick Boseman
was not in his 20s or 30s at the time of his

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.230
death.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:39.450
But that hardly lessens the sense that he
was another actor with many special performances

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:40.920
still in his future.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.470
Boseman's shocking death came at age 43, just
after films like "Black Panther" and "Avengers:

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:49.200
Infinity War" had made him a cornerstone of
the Marvel Cinematic Universe — he received

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.380
a colon cancer diagnosis in 2016 but had kept
it private.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.700
Boseman's first onscreen role was in a 2013
episode of "All My Children," which he followed

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.770
with guest roles in a plethora of other television
series.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:05.290
Boseman inched closer to the mainstream after
playing a trio of startlingly disparate, real-life

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:11.890
legends — Jackie Robinson in 2013's "42",
James Brown in 2014's "Get on Up", and Thurgood

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.890
Marshall in 2017's "Marshall".

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:17.940
But when he appeared as T'Challa in "Captain
America: Civil War" and "Black Panther", he

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.620
quickly became a household name.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:24.090
Posthumously, Boseman received his only Oscar
nomination for "Ma Rainey's Black Bottom",

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:28.040
and considerable award attention as well for
Spike Lee's "Da 5 Bloods".

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:33.130
Marvel fans received a bittersweet farewell
in 2021, as the Disney+ series "What If...?"

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.530
presented multiple episodes featuring voiceover
readings Boseman had done as T'Challa before

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.530
his passing.

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:43.240
Known primarily as a singer, Aaliyah's acting
career was just starting to manifest itself

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.870
when the actress died in a plane crash at
age 22.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.550
The accident occurred in August 2001, as the
"Try Again" singer was making her way home

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.090
from shooting a music video in the Bahamas.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:57.510
The beloved musician-turned-actress is still
talked about today — USA Today even called

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:02.180
Aaliyah "The Princess of R&amp;B" when they published
a piece to toast her legacy on the 20th anniversary

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:03.180
of her death.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.000
Aaliyah's experience was limited in terms
of acting, but her two film roles displayed

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.190
a magnetic on-screen presence, as well as
talent that seemed likely to blossom.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:15.260
She first tried her hand at acting in 2000's
"Romeo Must Die," in which she played a mobster's

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.840
daughter in love with a man from a rival mobster
family.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:22.232
Her other film role was as a vampire in the
horror flick "Queen of the Damned," released

00:11:22.232 --> 00:11:24.360
shortly after her death.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:28.470
Anton Yelchin was a well-known Hollywood actor
and star of well-regarded films like "Charlie

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:33.380
Bartlett" and blockbusters like the J.J. Abrams
"Star Trek" reboot movies — and then a terrible,

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:36.120
bizarre accident ended his life at the age
of 27.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:41.370
In June of 2016, Yelchin was killed when his
Jeep rolled backwards down his driveway, pinning

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.500
him between a brick pillar and a fence.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:47.380
The actor's family later filed a wrongful
death lawsuit against the vehicle maker, which

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:48.770
was settled out of court.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:52.880
The Russian-born actor was a recognizable
face thanks to shows like the Hank Azaria

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.000
series "Huff" and movies including "Alpha
Dog," "Terminator Salvation" and "The Beaver."

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.600
But it was his casting as Pavel Chekov that
seemed in the process of taking Yelchin's

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.120
career to the next level when he passed away.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:08.230
2016's "Star Trek Beyond" was dedicated in
his memory, and he had four other films released

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.230
posthumously.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:14.480
Back in the 1990s, it seemed impossible to
open a teen magazine that didn't feature Jonathan

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.280
Brandis somewhere within its pages.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:20.420
First starting with modeling and commercial
work, he eventually became a star at 14 when

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:24.320
he appeared in "The NeverEnding Story II:
The Next Chapter," and his leading role in

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.680
"SeaQuest DSV" solidified his teen heartthrob
status.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:32.100
Brandis' other notable work included the television
miniseries "It," and the films "Sidekicks"

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.620
and "Ladybugs."

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:37.260
Brandis had less success as an adult actor,
though he did have a few supporting roles

00:12:43.411 --> 00:12:47.501
Though Brandis did not leave a suicide note,
insiders told People magazine that he was

00:12:47.501 --> 00:12:51.611
depressed over the state of his career, and
had been drinking heavily prior to his death.

00:12:51.611 --> 00:12:56.221
If you or anyone you know is struggling with
addiction issues, help is available.

00:12:56.221 --> 00:13:00.301
Visit the Substance Abuse and Mental Health
Services Administration website or contact

00:13:00.301 --> 00:13:02.081
SAMHSA's National Helpline 

00:13:02.081 --> 00:13:06.041
at 1-800-662-HELP (4357).

00:13:06.041 --> 00:13:13.585
If you or someone you know is struggling with mental health, please contact the Crisis Text Line by texting HOME to 741741

00:13:13.585 --> 00:13:19.644
call the National Alliance on Mental Illness helpline at 1-800-950-NAMI (6264),

00:13:19.644 --> 00:13:22.792
 or visit the National Institute of Mental Health website.

00:13:22.792 --> 00:13:32.143
